## Discovering Rocket Science as Students

The Rocketry and Propulsion Division is where Sri Lankan university students gather to learn the physics of flight, build safe model rockets, and run digital aerodynamics simulations.

Since formal aerospace degrees are rare in Sri Lanka, our student club creates an accessible space for undergraduates to learn the fundamentals of rocketry through study circles, simulation workshops, and team experiments.

## What We Do in Our Club

### 1. Digital Flight Simulations
Before touching any physical hardware, we test our ideas on computers:
- Using OpenRocket to design virtual rocket bodies, nose cones, and fin layouts
- Learning how center of gravity and center of pressure affect stable flight
- Running simulations to see how wind, weight, and motor size impact maximum altitude

### 2. Rocket Physics and Propulsion Fundamentals
We organize peer learning sessions to understand how engines and fuels work:
- Studying the basic chemistry and thermodynamics of solid rocket propellants
- Understanding how nozzle shapes expand hot exhaust gases to generate thrust
- Learning aerospace safety guidelines and proper laboratory test protocols

### 3. Electronic Altimeters and Parachute Recovery
Getting a rocket safely back down is just as important as the launch:
- Building simple Arduino altimeter circuits that detect when a rocket reaches its peak height
- Sewing and testing small ripstop nylon parachutes for gentle landings
- Experimenting with radio transceivers to transmit live altitude readings back to a laptop

## What You Get as a Student Member

- Learn Aerospace Basics: Master simulation tools and flight physics that complement your university engineering and physics courses.
- Safety-First Practical Skills: Learn careful project planning, testing procedures, and teamwork.
- Supportive Student Community: Connect with fellow space enthusiasts who love discussing launch vehicles and interplanetary missions.
